{"id":"774a9c49-5db8-4e48-9d12-bb388787ccd3","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025997,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    names = [seg['name'] for seg in segments]\\n    importance = [seg['importance'] for seg in segments]\\n    min_alloc = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\\n    capacity = [seg['capacity'] for seg in segments]\\n    \\n    alloc = list(min_alloc)\\n    total_used = sum(alloc)\\n    \\n    if budget < total_used:\\n        raise ValueError('Budget below sum of minimum quotas')\\n    \\n    total_capacity = sum(capacity)\\n    if budget > total_capacity:\\n        raise ValueError('Budget exceeds total capacity')\\n    \\n    # greedy allocation\\n    while total_used < budget:\\n        best_idx = None\\n        best_score = -1\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if alloc[i] >= capacity[i]:\\n                continue\\n            # next token would be at position alloc[i] + 1 (1-indexed)\\n            score = importance[i] / (alloc[i] + 1)\\n            if score > best_score:\\n                best_score = score\\n                best_idx = i\\n        # must find one because budget <= total_capacity and not all full\\n        if best_idx is None:\\n            # should not happen due to budget check\\n            raise ValueError('Unexpected infeasibility')\\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\\n        total_used += 1\\n    \\n    # build output\\n    allocations = alloc\\n    by_name = {names[i]: alloc[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    total = budget\\n    return {'allocations': allocations, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements a greedy allocation algorithm as described. The case has \"segments\" list with each having \"name\", \"importance\", \"minimum\", \"capacity\". There is \"budget\" which is total tokens to allocate. The output must have: allocations (list of integers, same order as segments), by_name (object mapping segment name to integer allocation), total (integer sum of allocations). \n\nAlgorithm:\n- Initialize each segment allocation to its minimum.\n- Compute total used = sum of minimums.\n- If budget < total used: raise ValueError.\n- Total capacity = sum of capacities.\n- If budget > total capacity: raise ValueError.\n- Then while total used < budget:\n   - For each segment, compute score = importance / (current allocation + 1) ??? Wait: \"importance divided by its next allocated position\". Next allocated position means if currently have x tokens, next token would be at position x+1 (1-indexed). So score = importance / (current_allocation + 1). Because if we allocate the next token to that segment, its allocation becomes current+1, and the position of that token is current+1 (assuming tokens are assigned in order). \n   - Pick segment with max score. Use original segment order as tie-breaker (lower index first).\n   - Increase that segment's allocation by 1, and total_used by 1.\n- After loop, build allocations list in order of segments, by_name dict mapping name to allocation, total = budget.\n- Return dict with keys allocations, by_name, total.\n\nEdge cases: budget exactly equals sum of minimums -> no loop, allocations are all minima. budget exactly equals total capacity -> allocate each to its capacity (the greedy will eventually fill all to capacities because scores will become very small for filled segments but we must ensure we stop precisely when total used equals budget; if budget == total capacity, the while loop will run until each segment hits its capacity? But the greedy algorithm might not fill all because if a segment reaches capacity, its score becomes undefined? Actually when a segment reaches capacity, we cannot allocate more tokens to it, so we should exclude it from consideration. So while loop: consider only segments where current_allocation < capacity. Compute score for those. If no segment left and total_used < budget, then raise ValueError (budget > total capacity). But we already checked budget <= total capacity earlier. So fine.\n\nImplementation details: Use list of segment dicts. Keep arrays for allocations, capacities, importances, names. Use for loop to find best index each iteration.\n\nNeed to output as JSON-serializable object. Use standard Python.\n\nWe must not mutate case: make copies of relevant data.\n\nReturn exactly the dict. Raise ValueError if infeasible.\n\nNow write the code. The solve function signature: def solve(case). case is a dict with keys \"segments\" (list of dict) and \"budget\". Possibly other keys like \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nNote: The output schema says by_name dynamicKeysFrom case.segments[*].name. That means the keys are exactly the names from segments. So by_name must have keys equal to segment names. Our code will produce that.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with \"implementation\" key.\n\nMake sure to escape properly.\n\nI'll write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1499,"completion_tokens":1105,"total_tokens":2604,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":694},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":91},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}